::MCR_U06_L04_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
An object has mass 50 grams and volume 10 cubic cm. What is its density?
{
~A. 60 grams per cubic cm#This adds mass and volume. Density is mass divided by volume.
=B. 5 grams per cubic cm#Correct. Density = 50 divided by 10 = 5 grams per cubic cm.
~C. 500 grams per cubic cm#This multiplies mass and volume. Density requires division.
~D. 40 grams per cubic cm#This subtracts volume from mass. Use mass divided by volume.
}
::MCR_U06_L04_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
A material has density 6 grams per cubic cm and volume 8 cubic cm. What is the mass?
{
~A. 14 grams#This adds density and volume. Mass is density times volume.
~B. 1.33 grams#This divides volume by density. To find mass, multiply density by volume.
~C. 48 cubic cm#The number is correct, but mass should be labeled in grams.
=D. 48 grams#Correct. Mass = 6 x 8 = 48 grams.
}
::MCR_U06_L04_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
A sample has mass 36 grams and density 4 grams per cubic cm. What is its volume?
{
=A. 9 cubic cm#Correct. Volume = mass divided by density = 36 divided by 4 = 9 cubic cm.
~B. 40 cubic cm#This adds mass and density. Use division to find volume.
~C. 144 cubic cm#This multiplies mass and density. Volume is mass divided by density.
~D. 32 cubic cm#This subtracts density from mass. That does not use the density relationship.
}
::MCR_U06_L04_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
What does grams per cubic cm describe?
{
~A. length for each gram#This reverses the meaning. Density compares mass to volume.
~B. volume for each foot#This uses unrelated units. The unit compares grams and cubic cm.
=C. mass for each unit of volume#Correct. Grams per cubic cm tells how many grams are in each cubic centimeter.
~D. area for each unit of time#This does not match density units or the lesson concept.
}
::MCR_U06_L04_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L04_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.MCR.GM.4 through MLA.MCR.GM.6 and MLA.MCR.QR.2
Question:
Which formula finds density?
{
~A. mass x volume#This finds a product, not density. Density compares mass to volume by division.
=B. mass / volume#Correct. Density equals mass divided by volume.
~C. volume / mass#This reverses the ratio. Density is mass per unit volume.
~D. mass + volume#Adding does not create a ratio unit.
}
